      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but I'm a bit worried about the \"predictable\" part you mentioned. I don't want my script to feel like I'm just checking boxes.\n\nWhen you say Marcus and Andre fit the crime drama and Julian/Elena/Arthur fit the prestige drama, is that just a general vibe, or do you think their backgrounds and identities make them more \"natural\" fits for those specific worlds? Like, would a character like Marcus actually carry weight in a high-society setting, or does the storytelling feel more \"honest\" keeping him in the crime genre? I want to make sure I'm not making assumptions about where these people \"belong.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I think Marcus and Andre are more natural fits for the gritty crime drama because they're rooted in the harsh realities of the streets. They're not exactly the types who'd fit in with the high-society crowd, and their backgrounds would likely make them outsiders in that world. Julian, on the other hand, is a fish out of water in a gritty crime drama - his polished exterior and old-money background would make him a bit of an oddity in that world.\n\nElena and Arthur, on the other hand, are more likely to thrive in a high-society setting. Elena's refined taste and elegance would make her a natural fit for the world of high-end art and culture, and Arthur's obsession with legacy and reputation would make him a powerful player in that world.\n\nAs for Kenji, he's a bit of a wild card - his intelligence and discipline would make him a valuable asset in either world, but his quiet nature might make him more of an outsider in a high-society setting. Ultimately, it's up to you to decide where each character belongs, but I think it's worth considering their backgrounds and identities when making those decisions.\n\nIt's also worth noting that, in a high-society setting, characters like Marcus and",
